WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.964
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.964 --> 00:00:03.512
Wednesday the eleventh, Veterans Day.

00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:07.162
A mild, easy one, and the calm before a wet Thursday.

00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:09.711
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.711 --> 00:00:15.771
In a blind voice test, which clip do people tend to pick, and how do good arenas fix it?

00:00:15.771 --> 00:00:17.149
Answer's at the end.

00:00:17.149 --> 00:00:19.146
Weather's on your side today.

00:00:19.146 --> 00:00:21.694
Sunny, around sixty, barely any wind.

00:00:21.694 --> 00:00:24.036
Tomorrow's the one to plan around.

00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:32.920
A nor'easter rolls in, with the heaviest rain from about two to eight and gusts up in the thirties, so tomorrow's not a bike day.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:33.884
Okay, the day.

00:00:33.884 --> 00:00:35.950
Calendar has the gym at seven.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:39.876
Then it's got Godinger from nine to five, holiday or not.

00:00:39.876 --> 00:00:41.873
After that, nothing on there.

00:00:41.873 --> 00:00:46.625
A free night on a nice evening, which is worth using before the rain.

00:00:46.625 --> 00:00:47.589
Alright, news.

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:51.033
The Ukraine talks in Abu Dhabi actually got going.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:57.713
The Russian and Ukrainian sides spent about four hours across the table on Tuesday, with the U.S.

00:00:57.713 --> 00:00:58.677
in the middle.

00:00:58.677 --> 00:01:00.881
Zelensky called it constructive.

00:01:00.881 --> 00:01:01.432
The U.S.

00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:03.567
side says the gaps got smaller.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:12.864
Today's day two, and it's about energy plants, the Black Sea, and maybe a big prisoner swap, a thousand people each way, possibly more.

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:14.724
The hard part hasn't moved.

00:01:14.724 --> 00:01:20.027
Russia wants land Ukraine still holds, and it won't agree to a ceasefire yet.

00:01:20.027 --> 00:01:21.473
One from your corner.

00:01:21.473 --> 00:01:22.437
A Chinese A.I.

00:01:22.437 --> 00:01:30.220
company called MiniMax put out an image model you can download and run yourself, for making and editing pictures.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:37.382
They say it does 4K images in under ten seconds, and it can take up to sixteen reference images at once.

00:01:37.382 --> 00:01:41.308
That second part is the interesting one for product work.

00:01:41.308 --> 00:01:46.679
More reference shots means more chances to keep an object looking like itself.

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:49.090
And a nice one from the book world.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.949
The Booker Prize went to M.

00:01:50.949 --> 00:01:54.737
John Harrison for a novel called The End of Everything.

00:01:54.737 --> 00:02:04.172
He's eighty-one, the oldest winner ever, and it's the first time the prize has gone to someone who comes straight out of science fiction.

00:02:04.172 --> 00:02:12.919
The book is a funny, strange end-of-the-world story set on the English coast, with a slow invasion by shape-shifting creatures.

00:02:12.919 --> 00:02:14.847
Okay, here's one for Sunday.

00:02:14.847 --> 00:02:22.767
Bogart House, on Bogart Street right in Bushwick, is hosting something called Play Fair Brooklyn, from one to five.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:29.585
It's a fair for local clubs, more than twenty groups, and each one runs a little hands-on activity.

00:02:29.585 --> 00:02:33.993
Pottery, beading, knitting, chess, board games, some breathwork.

00:02:33.993 --> 00:02:36.128
Tickets are about thirty bucks.

00:02:36.128 --> 00:02:41.362
It's basically a sampler, and you might walk out with a new weeknight thing.

00:02:41.362 --> 00:02:45.425
Good fit for someone who likes making stuff with his hands.

00:02:45.425 --> 00:02:47.836
Today's challenge is for your body.

00:02:47.836 --> 00:02:51.555
Tonight, while you brush your teeth, stand on one leg.

00:02:51.555 --> 00:02:52.588
Switch halfway.

00:02:52.588 --> 00:02:58.786
If that's easy, close your eyes for the last few seconds and feel how much harder it gets.

00:02:58.786 --> 00:03:07.602
Balance fades quietly if you never test it, and it's the same thing that keeps you upright when the bike slides on a wet corner.

00:03:07.602 --> 00:03:10.150
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:17.725
Remember building the memo feed, and how an app only grabs the M.P.3 when the feed says there's a new episode?

00:03:17.725 --> 00:03:19.447
Here's what happens next.

00:03:19.447 --> 00:03:25.645
How your phone actually pulls down that file, and why you can skip ahead before it's done.

00:03:25.645 --> 00:03:29.364
Inside each episode in the feed there's the enclosure.

00:03:29.364 --> 00:03:30.880
It holds three things.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.769
The link to the file, what kind of file it is, and its length in bytes.

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:42.863
That length tells the app how big the download is, so it can show progress and check it got everything.

00:03:42.863 --> 00:03:44.240
Now the clever part.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.061
When your app asks for the file, it doesn't have to ask for all of it.

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:53.400
It can say, just send me bytes one million through two million.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.466
That's called a range request.

00:03:55.466 --> 00:03:58.083
The server sends back only that slice.

00:03:58.083 --> 00:03:59.874
That's how skipping works.

00:03:59.874 --> 00:04:03.317
Jump to minute three, and the app does quick math.

00:04:03.317 --> 00:04:09.653
Bytes per second, times the number of seconds, roughly where minute three lives in the file.

00:04:09.653 --> 00:04:16.403
It asks for that slice and starts playing from there, without downloading the first three minutes.

00:04:16.403 --> 00:04:19.571
It's also how a download survives a dead zone.

00:04:19.571 --> 00:04:24.185
Lose signal halfway down the block, and the app doesn't start over.

00:04:24.185 --> 00:04:28.317
It asks for the rest, starting at the byte where it stopped.

00:04:28.317 --> 00:04:31.210
The catch is the server has to support it.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:36.857
Most static hosting, including the kind serving this memo, does it out of the box.

00:04:36.857 --> 00:04:43.606
A server that can't would force every skip to wait, and every dropped download to start from zero.

00:04:43.606 --> 00:04:48.634
So: calendar says gym at seven and Godinger till five, then a free night.

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:50.149
Try the one-leg brush.

00:04:50.149 --> 00:04:55.590
The answer: usually the clip they heard last, so good arenas shuffle the order.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.000
Enjoy the sun while it lasts, Ziad.
